  "versions": [
    {
      "meaning": "Maturity and readiness for service",
      "description": "The number thirty marks maturity and readiness for service. Priests began their service at the age of thirty. David became king over Israel when he was thirty. Joseph was thirty when he entered Pharaoh's service. Jesus began his public ministry when he was about thirty years old.",
      "reason": "[minor] The description is correct and well-phrased, but lacks Bible references in the footnotes that could strengthen credibility. The biblical examples (priests at 30 years old, David, Joseph, Jesus) are all accurate, but it would have been useful to include references.; [minor] Judas' 30 pieces of silver is a well-known biblical reference linked to the number 30 that could be mentioned in a footnote, as it represents another dimension of the number (the price for a slave/betrayal price).; [minor] A footnote about the rabbinic tradition regarding 30 as the age of maturity would enrich the content without breaking the flow of the description.",
      "score": 7,
      "date": "2026-04-04"
    }
  ],
  "footnotes": [
    {
      "text": "In rabbinic tradition, thirty is regarded as the age of full strength (koach), as expressed in Pirke Avot 5:21: \"Thirty years – for full strength.\" This supports the biblical pattern where service begins at this age.",
      "source": "rabbinsk"
    },
    {
      "text": "Thirty silver coins were the price for a slave according to the Mosaic Law (Exod 21:32). The prophet Zechariah uses this amount ironically to describe the low value the people placed on the Lord (Zech 11:12–13), a text that in the New Testament is linked to Judas' betrayal price (Matt 26:15; 27:9).",
      "source": "teologisk"
    }
  ]
}
